\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
%
% RULE RConjSentVar
< m1: VERBPPROP{VERBPPROPrec1}
               [ I1:mu1,
                 predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}[mu2],
                 mu3 
               ]

  m2: T1
>

< m : VERBPPROP{VERBPPROPrec1}
               [ leftdislocrel/T1,
                 I1:mu1,
                 predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}[mu2],
                 I3:mu3
               ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: NOT EXIST (mu1, [leftdislocrel/..])
       I3: NOT EXIST (mu3, [I31:rel1/..])
         I31: rel1 IN [tempadvrel, locadvrel, postsentadvrel]
      >
<
   SUBRULE (* 1. SENTENCE *)
      < T1: SENTENCEVAR{SENTENCEVARrec1} >
      < T1: SENTENCEVAR{SENTENCEVARrec1} >

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       T1: SENTENCEVARrec1.adverbial = true AND
           SENTENCEVARrec1.temporal = false
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >

   SUBRULE (* 2. PREPP *)
      < T1: PREPPVAR{PREPPVARrec1} >
      < T1: PREPPVAR{PREPPVARrec1} >

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       T1: PREPPVARrec1.synppefs * [synNP, synOPENGERUND] <> []
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
>
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RConjSentVar
\item[* relevant examples:] Without having warned us, he left; In order to read 
the letter, she put on her glasses, etc
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] verbppropformation
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) RC\_Advvar
\item[* date of creation:] 2-NOV-1988
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] Dutch conjsvar
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 23/10/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
Adapted rule so that it will work in a large iterative class containing all 
advvar insertion rules. Especially:\\
1) there is only one var-extraction order in analysis (i.e.\ tempadv - locadv - 
sentadv), correlated with the actual surface order of the vars (which is 
sentadv (momentarily only in leftdislocrel) - locadv - tempadv), so there is 
right to left extraction\\
2) in generation, there are more orders possible for insertion (depending on 
source language)\\
3) the surface order for elements of the same class remains the same over 
languages, with obligatory left to right insertion (locadv1 - locadv2 - locadv3 
etc) = right to left extraction for locadvs and conjsents/sentadvs, 
and the reverse for tempadvs.
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] to introduce a variable for an adverbial subsentence in initial 
position.
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals) Only one 
leftdislocrel is allowed, although this is an iterative rule class. Note that 
the var-insertion rules are mapped on all other varinsertion rules in IL, 
irrespective of position. The substitution rules do have a preference, however: 
initial and middle conjsents are mapped onto initial ones, and final conjsents 
on final ones. To be able to translate a Dutch sentence which has both a 
leftdislocrel and a middle conjsent, middle conjsents may also be substituted 
in final position, but with a lowering of the bonus.
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:] one subrule for 
every case: sentences (because she loved him) and prepps (without warning us)
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\

\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}

