\begin{verbatim}

% RULE RFinalConjsentVar
< m1: VERBPPROP{VERBPPROPrec1}[mu1] 

  m2: T1
>

< m : VERBPPROP{VERBPPROPrec1}
            [ I2:mu1,
              postsentadvrel/T1
            ] 
>


   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I2: NOT EXIST(mu1, [I21:rel1/..])
         I21: rel1 IN [locadvrel, tempadvrel]
      >

<
   SUBRULE (* 1. SENTENCE *)
      < T1: SENTENCEVAR{SENTENCEVARrec1} >
      < T1: SENTENCEVAR{SENTENCEVARrec1} >

      COMP
         <
          C1: SENTENCEVARrec1.adverbial = true AND
              SENTENCEVARrec1.temporal  = false
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: SENTENCEVARrec1.adverbial = true AND
              SENTENCEVARrec1.temporal  = false
          A1: @
         >

   SUBRULE (* 2. PREPP *)
      < T1: PREPPVAR{PREPPVARrec1} >
      < T1: PREPPVAR{PREPPVARrec1} >

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       T1: PREPPVARrec1.synppefs * [synNP, synOPENGERUND] <> []
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
>
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RFinalConjsentVar
\item[* relevant examples:] we saw your new neighbours, although they don't live 
here yet; You will meet our best candidate, without considering all the details of 
her application.
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] VerbpPropFormation
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) RC\_SentAdvVar
\item[* date of creation:] 9-MAR-1989 
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] RIdentConjsentVar
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 23/10/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
Adapted rule so that it will work in a large iterative class containing all 
advvar insertion rules. Especially:\\
1) there is only one var-extraction order in analysis (i.e.\ tempadv - locadv - 
sentadv), correlated with the actual surface order of the vars (which is 
sentadv (momentarily only in leftdislocrel) - locadv - tempadv), so there is 
right to left extraction\\
2) in generation, there are more orders possible for insertion (depending on 
source language)\\
3) the surface order for elements of the same class remains the same over 
languages, with obligatory left to right insertion (locadv1 - locadv2 - locadv3 
etc) = right to left extraction for locadvs and conjsent/sentadvs, and the 
reverse for tempadvs. 
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] to introduce a variable for an adverbial subsentence in final 
position
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals) More than one
postsentadvrel is allowed .
Note that 
the var-insertion rules are mapped on all other varinsertion rules in IL, 
irrespective of position. The substitution rules do have a preference, however: 
initial and middle conjsents are mapped onto initial ones, and final conjsents 
on final ones. To be able to translate a Dutch sentence which has both a 
leftdislocrel and a middle conjsent, middle conjsents may also be substituted 
in final position, but with a lowering of the bonus.
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\
Dutch assumed an attribute {\em distri\/}(bution) for CONJs, indicating 
whether a CONJ can occur sentence initially and/or finally. In the substitution
rules (where the CONJ is visible), there is a test on correct occurrence. For
English, no examples could be found of CONJs restricted to final use (they all 
seem to be allowed initially), so this condition has not been built in.
\item rules:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\
Note that the ordering of conjsents may be relevant for pronominal reference; 
in case there is a `mismatch' between position in English and Dutch, there 
might be a problem when interpreting the correct reference.
\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}

\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
% RULE RSentadvVar
< m1: VERBPPROP{VERBPPROPrec1}
               [ I1:mu1,
                 predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}[mu2],
                 mu3 
               ]

  m2: I1::ADVPVAR{ADVPVARrec1}                 
>

< m : VERBPPROP{VERBPPROPrec1}
               [ leftdislocrel/I2::ADVPVAR{ADVPVARrec1},
                 I1:mu1,
                 predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}[mu2],
                 I3:mu3 
               ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: NOT EXIST (mu1, [leftdislocrel/..])
       I2: ADVPVARrec1.actsubcefs * [sentadv, causadv] <> []
       I3: NOT EXIST (mu3, [I31:rel1/..])
         I31: rel1 IN [locadvrel, tempadvrel, postsentadvrel]
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RSentadvVar
\item[* relevant examples:] Probably, obviously, hopefully, naturally; 
Therefore, (he left), Thus,
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] verbppropformation
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) RC\_Advvar
\item[* date of creation:] 2-NOV-1988 
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] Dutch Rsadvvar
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 23/10/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
Adapted rule so that it will work in a large iterative class containing all 
advvar insertion rules. Especially:\\
1) there is only one var-extraction order in analysis (i.e.\ tempadv - locadv - 
sentadv), correlated with the actual surface order of the vars (which is 
sentadv (momentarily only in leftdislocrel) - locadv - tempadv), so there is 
right to left extraction\\
2) in generation, there are more orders possible for insertion (depending on 
source language)\\
3) the surface order for elements of the same class remains the same over 
languages, with obligatory left to right insertion (locadv1 - locadv2 - locadv3 
etc) = right to left extraction for locadvs and conjsent/sentads, and the 
reverse for tempadvs.
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] to introduce a variable for sentence adverbials
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals) The advp does 
not have an attribute for position yet. It is assumed that in the Substitution Rule
for SentAdvs the adv is moved to position 2 (3?; following the auxiliary) if 
the position set allows it, or remains in sentence-initial position (if 1 is in 
the position set) and is given a comma immediately after it: Probably, he had 
left (vs. He had probably left by then (or: he probably had left by then?)). 
Note that there is only one leftdislocrel allowed, although the rule class is 
iterative.
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
